menu "WiFi Manager"

    config MAX_AP_COUNT
        int "Maximum stored AP credentials"
        default 5
        range 1 20
        help
            Capacity of the credential store (ap_records). Conceptually
            owned by the ap_records module rather than wifi_manager proper
            (see 03_configuration.md), but exposed here because ap_records
            is vendored directly into this component's src/ directory
            rather than built as a separate IDF component with its own
            Kconfig.

            Must satisfy CONFIG_WIFI_SCAN_MAX_RESULTS >= CONFIG_MAX_AP_COUNT
            (enforced by a _Static_assert at build time) or stored
            credentials may be silently skipped during scan matching.

            Raising this value increases wifi_mgr task stack usage — see
            CONFIG_WIFI_TASK_STACK_SIZE help text and the stack budget
            table in 03_configuration.md.

    config WIFI_KNOWN_AP_WAIT_MS
        int "Time window (tw) duration in milliseconds"
        default 120000
        range 5000 300000
        help
            Hard deadline from first live AP detection to BLE provisioning start.
            All scan cycles and connection attempts must complete within this window.
            Formula for minimum safe value:
            tw >= MAX_AP_COUNT * MAX_CONNECT_ATTEMPTS * CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MS
            At defaults (5 * 3 * 8000 = 120000 ms). Increase for environments
            with many stored APs, slow WPA2-Enterprise auth, or high-latency APs.

    config WIFI_INTER_SCAN_DELAY_MS
        int "Inter-scan delay in milliseconds"
        default 3000
        range 500 30000
        help
            Delay between end of scan result processing and next scan start.
            Actual scan cadence = inter_scan_delay_ms + scan_duration (~2s).
            At defaults: ~5s per cycle.

    config WIFI_SCAN_MAX_RESULTS
        int "Maximum scan results per cycle"
        default 20
        range 1 20
        help
            Cap on AP records fetched per scan cycle. APs beyond this cap are
            silently dropped. Must satisfy two independent lower bounds:
            (1) >= CONFIG_MAX_AP_COUNT (or stored credentials may be skipped)
            (2) >= number of APs simultaneously visible in the densest deployment
            environment (or stored APs with weaker signal may be crowded out).
            See L2 in 01_overview.md.

    config WIFI_SCAN_HIDDEN
        bool "Scan for hidden SSIDs"
        default n
        help
            Enable to include hidden networks in scan results.
            Required for auto-reconnect to hidden SSIDs after provisioning.
            See L1 in 01_overview.md.

    config WIFI_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MS
        int "Per-AP connection attempt timeout in milliseconds"
        default 8000
        range 2000 30000
        help
            How long to wait for GOT_IP before considering a connection attempt
            failed and moving to the next retry or next AP.

    config WIFI_MAX_CONNECT_ATTEMPTS
        int "Maximum connection attempts per AP"
        default 3
        range 1 10
        help
            Number of times to attempt connecting to each AP before advancing
            to the next AP in the connect list. First attempt uses BSSID lock
            (if BSSID is stored); subsequent attempts use SSID-only.

    choice WIFI_AUTO_PROVISION
        prompt "Automatic BLE provisioning on tw expiry"
        default WIFI_AUTO_PROVISION_WHEN_UNPROVISIONED
        help
            Decides whether the component may start BLE provisioning by
            itself when the tw window (WIFI_KNOWN_AP_WAIT_MS) expires without
            a connection. Runtime override: wifi_mgr_config_t.auto_provision.
            wifi_mgr_start_ble_provisioning() works under every option.

            When provisioning is not allowed, tw expiry posts
            WIFI_MGR_EVENT_NO_KNOWN_AP_IN_TW or
            WIFI_MGR_EVENT_KNOWN_AP_UNREACHABLE, re-arms the window and keeps
            scanning. It never disconnects and never discards a connection
            that completes at the same moment.

        config WIFI_AUTO_PROVISION_WHEN_UNPROVISIONED
            bool "Only when no credentials are stored (recommended)"
            help
                First-boot devices provision automatically. A device that
                already holds credentials never does: a router that takes
                longer than tw to reboot, with neighbours' APs in range,
                no longer drops it into BLE provisioning (and into
                accepting credentials from anyone in BLE range). The
                stored-credential count is checked when tw expires, so
                removing every credential re-enables it.

                To add a network to a device that already has credentials
                (moved, router replaced, password changed), the application
                must call wifi_mgr_reboot_into_setup() from its own setup
                trigger — a button, an app command, a power-cycle gesture.

        config WIFI_AUTO_PROVISION_ALWAYS
            bool "On every tw expiry (0.1.x / 0.2.x behaviour)"
            help
                Kept for compatibility. Not recommended for devices that
                stay installed: see the WHEN_UNPROVISIONED help text. With
                credentials stored, each automatic setup mode is limited by
                WIFI_SETUP_DEADLINE_MS, so a router outage alternates between
                scanning and setup mode instead of staying in setup mode.

                Do not combine with WIFI_PROV_BT_LIFECYCLE_FREE_ON_SUCCESS:
                each automatic setup mode then costs a reboot (see that
                option's help text).

        config WIFI_AUTO_PROVISION_NEVER
            bool "Never — only via wifi_mgr_start_ble_provisioning()"
            help
                For products that always have a physical provisioning
                trigger (button, power-cycle gesture). Note that a
                first-boot device then sits in scanning until the
                application calls wifi_mgr_start_ble_provisioning().
    endchoice

    choice WIFI_PROV_AUTH_MODE
        prompt "Provisioning security mode"
        default WIFI_PROV_AUTH_MODE_MAC_POP
        help
            Selects the protocomm security level and proof-of-possession
            (PoP) source used during BLE provisioning. The PoP is what the
            BLE Prov app / esp_prov script prompts the operator for before
            it will accept WiFi credentials.

            ESP-IDF v6.0 note: protocomm security versions 0 and 1 are
            disabled by default in v6.0. Each option below automatically
            selects the matching CONFIG_ESP_PROTOCOMM_SUPPORT_SECURITY_VERSION_x
            symbol; without it, network_prov_mgr_start_provisioning() fails
            at runtime with ESP_ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED and no log output.

            SECURITY_1 is Curve25519 key exchange + AES-CTR with the PoP as
            the shared secret (not SRP6a — that is SECURITY_2).

        config WIFI_PROV_AUTH_MODE_OPEN
            bool "Open — no authentication (SECURITY_0)"
            select ESP_PROTOCOMM_SUPPORT_SECURITY_VERSION_0
            help
                No PoP, no encryption of provisioning traffic. Anyone
                within BLE range can provision the device. Use only for
                bench testing or trusted/shielded environments.

        config WIFI_PROV_AUTH_MODE_STATIC_POP
            bool "Static PoP, set via CONFIG_WIFI_PROV_POP (SECURITY_1)"
            select ESP_PROTOCOMM_SUPPORT_SECURITY_VERSION_1
            help
                Use a fixed PoP string compiled into every unit, set below
                via CONFIG_WIFI_PROV_POP. This is the right choice for
                headless devices: there is no console to read a
                MAC-derived PoP from, so the integrator chooses (and
                distributes to installers/provisioning tooling) a known
                value ahead of time instead.

                Same PoP on every unit of a given firmware build — treat
                it like a shared secret, not a per-device credential.

        config WIFI_PROV_AUTH_MODE_MAC_POP
            bool "MAC-derived PoP, logged to console (SECURITY_1, recommended)"
            select ESP_PROTOCOMM_SUPPORT_SECURITY_VERSION_1
            help
                PoP is derived from the device's own MAC address and
                printed to the serial console at provisioning start (see
                wifi_mgr_prov.c). Unique per device with zero extra
                configuration, but requires the operator to have console
                access to read the value — not suitable for headless
                devices with no exposed UART/log output.
    endchoice

    config WIFI_PROV_POP
        string "Provisioning proof-of-possession (PoP) string"
        default "abcd1234"
        help
            Static PoP used when CONFIG_WIFI_PROV_AUTH_MODE_STATIC_POP is
            selected above. Ignored (but still compiled in) for the Open
            and MAC-derived modes.

            For headless devices: set this in your project's
            sdkconfig.defaults to a value known ahead of time by whatever
            tooling or installers will provision units in the field, e.g.
                CONFIG_WIFI_PROV_AUTH_MODE_STATIC_POP=y
                CONFIG_WIFI_PROV_POP="CHANGEME-1234"
            replacing CHANGEME-1234 with your own value before flashing —
            do not ship the placeholder as-is.

            Must be under WIFI_MGR_PROV_POP_BUF_SIZE (64) characters; this
            is enforced by a _Static_assert in wifi_mgr_prov.c, so an
            oversized value fails the build rather than being silently
            truncated.

            Kept unconditional (no "depends on") so it is always available
            to read at runtime regardless of which mode is active —
            simpler than gating it and risking an undefined symbol if the
            mode is switched without also touching this value.

    config WIFI_PROV_DEVICE_NAME_PREFIX
        string "BLE device name prefix for provisioning"
        default "PROV_"
        help
            Prefix prepended to the MAC suffix to form the BLE advertisement
            name. E.g. "PROV_" + "AABBCC" = "PROV_AABBCC".

    config WIFI_PROV_TIMEOUT_MS
        int "Provisioning attempt timeout in milliseconds (0 = no timeout)"
        default 120000
        range 0 600000
        help
            How long one BLE provisioning attempt (one advertise/connect
            cycle) may run before it is counted as failed and restarted in
            place.

            How this relates to the setup deadline:
            - No credentials stored: only this timeout applies. Attempts
              restart in place until provisioning succeeds.
            - Credentials stored: this limits each attempt, and an expired
              attempt restarts in place; WIFI_SETUP_DEADLINE_MS limits the
              whole setup mode. Whichever ends first wins. The deadline also
              applies when this value is 0.

    config WIFI_SETUP_DEADLINE_MS
        int "Setup mode deadline when credentials are stored (ms)"
        default 300000
        range 30000 3600000
        help
            When the device already holds credentials, setup mode (BLE
            provisioning entered via wifi_mgr_reboot_into_setup(),
            wifi_mgr_start_ble_provisioning() or tw expiry) ends after this
            long and the device returns to scanning, reconnecting to a stored
            network. Prevents an accidental or abandoned setup request from
            leaving a working device in setup mode.

            Failed attempts (wrong password, attempt timeout) inside the
            deadline restart in place, so a typo does not end setup mode.
            Has no effect when no credentials are stored.

    config WIFI_SETUP_GRACE_MS
        int "Setup deadline extension while in use (ms)"
        default 60000
        range 10000 600000
        help
            If the setup deadline is reached while a phone is connected over
            BLE, or while received credentials are being tried, the deadline
            is extended by this much (repeatedly), up to WIFI_SETUP_MAX_MS.

    config WIFI_SETUP_MAX_MS
        int "Setup mode hard maximum when credentials are stored (ms)"
        default 900000
        range 30000 7200000
        help
            Absolute cap on setup mode when credentials are stored, including
            grace extensions. Values below WIFI_SETUP_DEADLINE_MS are raised
            to it at run time.

            At the cap, setup ends even if an attempt is in progress: the
            received credentials are discarded (nothing is saved, even if the
            connection succeeds at that moment), the station is disconnected
            and confirmed disconnected, the driver's copy is wiped, and the
            device returns to scanning.

    choice WIFI_PROV_BT_LIFECYCLE
        prompt "Bluetooth memory after successful provisioning"
        default WIFI_PROV_BT_LIFECYCLE_KEEP_ALIVE
        help
            What happens to Bluetooth memory after a successful provisioning
            session.

            Background (verified against ESP-IDF v6.0 + network_provisioning
            1.3.x with NimBLE and Bluedroid): the BLE controller is NOT kept
            running between sessions under either option. Every session stop
            tears it down (protocomm_ble_stop() -> nimble_port_deinit() ->
            esp_bt_controller_disable()/deinit()), and every session start
            initializes it again; that cycle is supported. The one step that
            cannot be undone within a boot is esp_bt_mem_release(), which
            returns the memory reserved for Bluetooth to the heap. After it,
            BLE cannot be started again until a reboot.

            Earlier versions of this help text said the controller "stays
            initialized" under KEEP_ALIVE and that controller deinit/reinit
            was the irreversible step. Both were wrong; behaviour is unchanged.

        config WIFI_PROV_BT_LIFECYCLE_KEEP_ALIVE
            bool "Keep Bluetooth memory reserved (recommended default)"
            help
                Bluetooth memory stays reserved for the rest of the boot, so
                setup mode can be entered again at any time without a reboot.
                Costs the reserved memory (documented as ~20-30 KB; measure
                free internal heap on your target before deciding).

        config WIFI_PROV_BT_LIFECYCLE_FREE_ON_SUCCESS
            bool "Release Bluetooth memory after success (re-entering setup reboots)"
            help
                After a successful session, network_prov_mgr is
                deinitialized and the FREE_BTDM scheme handler calls
                esp_bt_mem_release(), returning the reserved memory to the
                heap for the rest of the boot.

                Any later entry into setup mode in the same boot reboots the
                device first, through the same mechanism as
                wifi_mgr_reboot_into_setup(), so it comes back up directly in
                setup mode.

                Do not combine with WIFI_AUTO_PROVISION_ALWAYS: during a long
                router outage the device would cycle tw expiry -> reboot into
                setup -> setup deadline -> scanning -> tw expiry, i.e. one
                reboot every few minutes for as long as the router is down.
                A warning is logged at start-up if both are selected.
    endchoice

    config WIFI_PROV_BLE_RETRY_BACKOFF_MS
        int "Backoff before retrying a synchronously-failed BLE restart (ms)"
        default 2000
        range 500 30000
        help
            Used only by on_prov_failed()'s retry-storm guard: if
            network_prov_mgr_start_provisioning() itself fails synchronously
            while restarting an existing provisioning session in place
            (rather than failing via the normal CONFIG_WIFI_PROV_TIMEOUT_MS
            timeout path), nothing else throttles the next attempt — the
            prov event handler never fired, so no NOTIFY_PROV_FAILED is
            coming on its own. This value bounds that wait before retrying
            once more.

            Deliberately a separate value from CONFIG_WIFI_INTER_SCAN_DELAY_MS,
            even though both are "pace a retry" delays: WiFi scan pacing and
            BLE provisioning error recovery are unrelated operational
            concerns with no reason to share a tuning knob. Raising
            CONFIG_WIFI_INTER_SCAN_DELAY_MS for a battery-powered deployment's
            scan cadence should not also slow down recovery from this rare
            BLE error path, and vice versa.

            This path is rare in practice — it requires the underlying BLE
            stack to reject a start call outright, not just time out — so
            the default is intentionally short relative to most other
            timing values in this component.

    config WIFI_TASK_STACK_SIZE
        int "Manager task stack size in bytes"
        default 6144
        range 2048 16384
        help
            Stack size for the wifi_manager internal task.
            Must be large enough to accommodate:
            - scan buffer: CONFIG_WIFI_SCAN_MAX_RESULTS * 76 bytes
            - two ap_info_t arrays: 2 * CONFIG_MAX_AP_COUNT * sizeof(ap_info_t)
            - general function call overhead (~1KB)
            At defaults (SCAN_MAX_RESULTS=20, MAX_AP_COUNT=5): ~2720B peak
            inside wifi_mgr_scan_handle_results(), fits in 6144B with margin.
            Raise proportionally when increasing SCAN_MAX_RESULTS or MAX_AP_COUNT.

    config WIFI_TASK_PRIORITY
        int "Manager task priority"
        default 5
        range 1 20
        help
            FreeRTOS priority for the wifi_manager internal task.

    config WIFI_USE_COUNT_SAVE_INTERVAL
        int "Use-count NVS save interval (connections between saves)"
        default 5
        range 1 100
        help
            ap_records_save() is called after every Nth successful connection
            to throttle NVS write frequency. NVS writes block ~10-20ms.
            Lower values persist use_count changes more frequently at the
            cost of more frequent NVS writes. See L3 in 01_overview.md.

    choice WIFI_PS_MODE
        prompt "Wi-Fi power save"
        default WIFI_PS_MODE_MIN_MODEM
        help
            Applied after esp_wifi_start() and re-applied after every
            provisioning session. Runtime override: wifi_mgr_config_t.ps_mode.
            Applications using this component should not call
            esp_wifi_set_ps() themselves.

        config WIFI_PS_MODE_NONE
            bool "None (lowest latency)"
            help
                No modem sleep. Lowest receive latency, highest power. Use
                for audio and other latency-sensitive traffic.
        config WIFI_PS_MODE_MIN_MODEM
            bool "Minimum modem sleep (ESP-IDF default)"
            help
                Wakes every DTIM. Incoming data can wait up to one DTIM
                interval (typically ~100-300 ms).
        config WIFI_PS_MODE_MAX_MODEM
            bool "Maximum modem sleep (lowest power)"
            help
                Wakes per listen interval. Longest receive delay.
    endchoice

endmenu
